Trace to the daemon log, not to fd 2, and say who waited and who was signalled.

FD 2 IS NOT OURS. A spawning caller dup2s its own pipes over the child stdout and stderr before the
exec, so every CIDER_EXEC line the child printed after that went into the caller rather than the
log. That is not a cosmetic loss: it made a child that ran perfectly look like one that died
between fork and exec, and I recorded that wrong conclusion in the last iteration. Every line now
goes to __simple_kprintf, which reaches the daemon log whatever the process does to its
descriptors, and carries pid= so a shared log names its process.

MILESTONES INSIDE posix_spawn, because try= and a silence do not say how far the child got:
  spawn=parent pid=N          the fork returned in the parent
  spawn=child / childready    the child is alive / its libsystem hooks have run
  spawn=attrs flags=X acts=N  the attributes and the number of file actions
  spawn=act i=N type=T fd=F newfd=G   each action before it is performed
  spawn=attrsdone / preexec / fail code=N

TWO MORE SYSCALLS join the same switch, because the question moved past exec:
  wait=try for=PID opts=X  and  wait=ret ret=N status=X   in sys_wait4
  signal=N handler=P                                      in the signal wrapper

WHAT THEY MEASURED, on CMake configure (#235): the spawn of /usr/bin/uname runs to completion. All
five file actions execute, the exec happens, the parent wait4 returns the child with status 0. So
the spawn path is NOT the defect, and neither is SIGCHLD: the trace shows CMake never installs a
SIGCHLD handler at all, which /proc SigCgt confirms.

